    "title": "Cheque-bounce cases: Kuntegowda explains financial capacity and the limits of revision",
    "description": "Kuntegowda v. Thurubaiah, 2026 INSC 790: Section 138 conviction restored after scrutiny of cheque presumptions and the lender’s evidence.",
    "answer": "The Supreme Court restored the trial and Sessions Court orders in a cheque-dishonour prosecution. It held that the accused had not rebutted the statutory presumptions on the evidence and that the High Court had exceeded the proper limits of revision when overturning concurrent findings. (Judgment, paragraphs 6–8.)",
    "sections": [
      [
        "A loan, a dishonoured cheque and three different outcomes",
        "The dispute concerned a ₹4.5 lakh hand loan, allegedly advanced for purchase of a house site and repayable within one year with 16% annual interest. A cheque was returned for insufficient funds. The complainant issued a demand notice and brought a Section 138 complaint. The trial court convicted the accused and imposed a ₹9 lakh fine; the Sessions Court upheld conviction but reduced the fine to ₹6.5 lakh. The High Court then acquitted him in revision. (Paragraphs 2–3.7.)"
      ],
      [
        "How the presumptions operated",
        "The accused admitted his signature. With the statutory requirements satisfied, Sections 118 and 139 of the Negotiable Instruments Act shifted the evidentiary burden to him to rebut the presumption supporting the complainant. That did not make the presumption conclusive. The Court examined his challenge to the lender’s financial capacity, the investment evidence and the supporting witnesses. It found that the defence did not undermine the complainant’s account when the evidence was considered together. (Paragraphs 6–6.10.)"
      ],
      [
        "Why the High Court’s revision was set aside",
        "Revision is not simply another full appeal on the facts. The Court found no identified glaring contradiction or perversity sufficient to justify the High Court’s intervention. Re-examining testimony in detail did not establish a lawful reason to discard the concurrent decisions. The Supreme Court therefore restored the lower-court orders, including the Sessions Court’s modification of the fine. Describing the result merely as restoration of a ₹9 lakh fine would miss that appellate modification. (Paragraphs 3.5 and 7–8.)"
      ],
      [
        "The useful distinction for readers",
        "The ruling connects two questions that should remain separate: whether an accused has rebutted the statutory presumption and whether a revisional court may overturn the findings reached below. Financial capacity can be examined through evidence; this judgment does not make it irrelevant. Its conclusion was that the challenge failed on this record, and that the High Court had not supplied a sufficient basis for exercising its restricted revisional power. (Paragraphs 6–8.)"
      ]
    ],
    "question": "Does an admitted signature make conviction automatic?",
    "reply": "No. The presumptions remain rebuttable, and the statutory conditions and defence evidence still matter. The Court found those presumptions unrebutted in this case. (Paragraphs 6–6.10.)",
