    "title": "Medical-college subvention after TMA Pai: why the Supreme Court rejected a five-year payment claim",
    "description": "Government of India v. Sri Devraj Urs Medical College, 2026 INSC 799: annual subvention, the final TMA Pai decision and proof of expenditure.",
    "answer": "The Supreme Court partly allowed the governments’ appeal over medical-college subvention. It rejected the direction to continue payments for an entire five-year course for students admitted in 2002–03, examining both the effect of the final TMA Pai ruling and the absence of supporting financial pleadings. (Judgment, paragraphs 6–13.1.)",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Where the asserted entitlement came from",
        "An interim order made during the TMA Pai litigation had established a subvention scheme intended to address capitation-fee concerns. The college sought continued payments for students admitted while that scheme operated. The Karnataka High Court accepted a claim extending through course completion for the relevant cohort. The Supreme Court examined whether an interim arrangement could support that continuing obligation after the eleven-judge Bench’s final decision of 31 October 2002. (Paragraphs 1–5 and 8.)"
      ],
      [
        "Annual payments were not an upfront course guarantee",
        "The Court treated the scheme as imposing an annual obligation, rather than requiring payment for the entire course at once. It found that the final TMA Pai ruling displaced the subvention arrangement. The reference to a maximum five-year period did not, in the Court’s analysis, preserve every future annual instalment after the scheme ceased to operate. That distinction explains why an existing admission did not itself secure all later payments. (Paragraphs 8 and 12.2–12.3.)"
      ],
      [
        "What the clarification did—and did not—preserve",
        "The Court examined the 1 April 2003 clarificatory order and the provision for bringing existing legal arrangements into conformity with TMA Pai. It rejected the High Court’s reading of those directions as preserving the subvention scheme prospectively for the whole course. The judgment also discusses the general retrospective operation of judicial decisions unless prospectivity is expressly provided. That proposition is explained here within the dispute the Court actually decided. (Paragraphs 9–12.3.)"
      ],
      [
        "The missing financial evidence and the limited outcome",
        "The college had not supplied data answering whether tuition and other revenue met its running expenses. The Court identified that absence as another reason it could not sustain the payment direction. The appeal was partly allowed, preserving the denial of payments beyond academic year 2002–03 and setting aside the course-long direction for the 2002–03 entrants. This is a decision about a specific historical scheme, not a general prohibition on all public support for private medical education. (Paragraphs 6–6.2 and 13–14, including direction A following paragraph 13.1.)"
      ]
    ],
    "question": "Did admission in 2002–03 create an unconditional right to five years of subvention?",
    "reply": "The Court rejected that reading: payments were annual, and the interim scheme did not continue unchanged after the final TMA Pai decision. (Paragraphs 12.2–13.1.)",
